Gossip Protocol — Distributed Failure Detector
A peer-to-peer failure detection system built from scratch in Python, implementing the core mechanics of gossip protocols used in distributed databases like Cassandra and DynamoDB.
What it does

Each node maintains a local membership list — its belief about every other node in the network
Nodes periodically gossip their membership list to a random neighbour over UDP
Information spreads epidemically — a node learns about the entire network without ever connecting to it directly
A watchdog thread on each node independently detects failures based on heartbeat staleness

How failure detection works
Each membership list entry tracks a heartbeat counter and a last_updated timestamp. The timestamp is always set from the local clock — never trusted from the network. If a node's heartbeat stops increasing, the watchdog marks it SUSPECTED after T_suspect seconds and DEAD after T_fail seconds.
Running it
bashpython main.py
Change N at the top of the file to run with any number of nodes. The network topology is generated automatically — a ring for guaranteed connectivity plus random extra edges for faster convergence.
Key design decisions

State is external — a node cannot declare itself alive or dead. That judgment belongs to its observers.
Local clocks only — last_updated reflects when you observed a heartbeat change, not when it happened.
UDP over TCP — gossip is fire and forget. No handshake, no guarantee, no overhead.
Deep copy before send — gossip snapshots are isolated from the live membership list.
Thread per responsibility — heartbeat, sender, receiver, and watchdog run independently per node.

Concepts demonstrated
Eventual consistency, epidemic information propagation, decentralised failure detection, concurrent UDP networking, thread safety with locks.